      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration",
      "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-6,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot x\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-6 \\\\  & =8(3-1)-6 \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2-6 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
